Introduction to Apache JMeter
Apache JMeter is an open-source Java-based application used for load testing and
performance testing. It can test web applications, HTTP/HTTPS services, REST APIs,
SOAP services, JDBC databases, FTP, LDAP, JMS, and other supported protocols.
JMeter primarily works at the protocol level; it is not a normal browser and does not
execute client-side JavaScript like a browser.
Important JMeter Terminology
1 Test Plan
A Test Plan is the top-level container of a JMeter test. It can contain Thread Groups,
Samplers, Listeners, Timers, Assertions, and other elements.
2 Thread
A Thread represents a virtual user. For example, Number of Threads = 20 means JMeter
simulates 20 virtual users.
3 Thread Group
A Thread Group controls the number of users, Ramp-Up Period, Loop Count, and the
action to take after a sampler error.
4 Ramp-Up Time
Ramp-Up Time determines how quickly JMeter starts all configured users. Example: 20
users with a 10-second ramp-up means the users are started over a 10-second period.
5 Loop Count
Loop Count determines how many times each virtual user repeats the configured test
sequence. Example: 20 users × 5 loops = 100 executions when one request is inside the
loop.
